diff --git a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-01-02-imperfecto-vs-indefinido-gr.json b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-01-02-imperfecto-vs-indefinido-gr.json
index 7c12b545..911f6ee0 100644
--- a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-01-02-imperfecto-vs-indefinido-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-01-02-imperfecto-vs-indefinido-gr.json
@@ -93,6 +93,61 @@
         }
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "content": "You know *estar* from A1 Unit 9 and the *imperfecto* from A2 Unit 21. New here is *estar a punto de* + infinitive: it says that something was about to happen, and the sentence goes on with the event that came at that moment. In a story it is mostly in the *imperfecto*, and the event that interrupts it is in the *indefinido*: *Estaba a punto de salir cuando sonó el teléfono.* The action itself had not happened yet. The same pattern works in the present for what is about to happen now: *Estoy a punto de salir.*"
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Estar a punto de + infinitive (imperfecto)",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "yo estaba a punto de salir",
+          "I was about to leave"
+        ],
+        [
+          "tú estabas a punto de salir",
+          "you were about to leave"
+        ],
+        [
+          "él, ella, usted estaba a punto de salir",
+          "he, she was / you (formal) were about to leave"
+        ],
+        [
+          "nosotros estábamos a punto de salir",
+          "we were about to leave"
+        ],
+        [
+          "vosotros estabais a punto de salir",
+          "you all were about to leave (Spain)"
+        ],
+        [
+          "ellos, ellas, ustedes estaban a punto de salir",
+          "they, you all were about to leave"
+        ]
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "Estaba a punto de salir cuando sonó el teléfono.",
+          "english": "I was about to leave when the phone rang."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "El autobús estaba a punto de llegar cuando empezó a llover.",
+          "english": "The bus was about to arrive when it started to rain."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Estábamos a punto de comer cuando llegó mi hermano.",
+          "english": "We were about to eat when my brother arrived."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Ahora estoy a punto de salir.",
+          "english": "I am about to leave now."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
       "content": "Ask whether the verb is the stage or the action on it. The stage (weather, time, feelings, what was in progress) takes the *imperfecto*; the action takes the *indefinido*. For past habits the *imperfecto* is also used: *íbamos*, *leía*."
diff --git a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-02-02-indefinido-experiencias-gr.json b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-02-02-indefinido-experiencias-gr.json
index 71dbde08..d939b462 100644
--- a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-02-02-indefinido-experiencias-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-02-02-indefinido-experiencias-gr.json
@@ -27,6 +27,53 @@
         }
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "content": "You know the gerund from A1 Unit 19 (*estoy comiendo*, *estoy leyendo*). Without *estar*, the gerund also says how or while the main verb happens: it names what the same person is doing at the same moment. *Llegó corriendo* means \"she arrived running\", *Volvimos a casa cantando* means \"we went home singing\". The gerund never changes: the main verb carries the person and the tense."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "The gerund: one form for every person",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "hablar → hablando",
+          "speaking"
+        ],
+        [
+          "correr → corriendo",
+          "running"
+        ],
+        [
+          "salir → saliendo",
+          "leaving"
+        ],
+        [
+          "leer → leyendo",
+          "reading (irregular: -yendo)"
+        ]
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "Llegó corriendo al auditorio.",
+          "english": "She arrived at the auditorium running."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Volvimos a casa cantando después del concierto.",
+          "english": "We went back home singing after the concert."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Salí del escenario sonriendo.",
+          "english": "I left the stage smiling."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Mi amiga habló mirando al público.",
+          "english": "My friend spoke looking at the audience."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
       "content": "Choose the *indefinido* when the listener can locate the experience as one completed episode, even if its effect lasts. The *imperfecto* stays for the background (*estaba nervioso*, *guardaba silencio*)."
diff --git a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-05-04-relative-clauses-gr.json b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-05-04-relative-clauses-gr.json
index 579c37aa..d7db6850 100644
--- a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-05-04-relative-clauses-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-05-04-relative-clauses-gr.json
@@ -27,6 +27,53 @@
         }
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "content": "You know possessives such as *mi* and *su* from A1. Inside a relative clause, *cuyo* does their job and means \"whose\": it joins the owner (*la amiga*) to the thing owned (*el hermano*). It agrees in gender and number with the thing owned, not with the owner, and no article follows it: *la amiga cuyo hermano vive en Madrid*, because *hermano* is masculine singular."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Cuyo agrees with the thing owned",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "cuyo + masculine singular: la amiga cuyo hermano vive en Madrid",
+          "the friend whose brother lives in Madrid"
+        ],
+        [
+          "cuya + feminine singular: el vecino cuya hija estudia música",
+          "the neighbour whose daughter studies music"
+        ],
+        [
+          "cuyos + masculine plural: la familia cuyos hijos viven cerca",
+          "the family whose children live nearby"
+        ],
+        [
+          "cuyas + feminine plural: la vecina cuyas amigas viven cerca",
+          "the neighbour whose friends live nearby"
+        ]
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "Tengo una amiga cuyo hermano vive en Madrid.",
+          "english": "I have a friend whose brother lives in Madrid."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "El vecino cuya hija estudia música siempre nos saluda.",
+          "english": "The neighbour whose daughter studies music always greets us."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Conozco a una familia cuyos hijos comparten muchas actividades con los vecinos.",
+          "english": "I know a family whose children share many activities with the neighbours."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Mi vecina, cuyas amigas viven cerca, nos ayuda mucho.",
+          "english": "My neighbour, whose friends live nearby, helps us a lot."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
       "content": "The relative clause normally follows the noun it describes. Choose the relative form according to its role in the clause, not simply according to the noun before it."
diff --git a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-12-05-integrated-explanation-and-narrative-gr.json b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-12-05-integrated-explanation-and-narrative-gr.json
index 1612b958..fd332d91 100644
--- a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-12-05-integrated-explanation-and-narrative-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-12-05-integrated-explanation-and-narrative-gr.json
@@ -27,6 +27,61 @@
         }
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "content": "You know the *indefinido* from A2 Unit 11 and the *imperfecto* from A2 Unit 21. New here is *dejar de* + infinitive: it means to stop doing something you did regularly, such as a habit. *Dejé de fumar hace dos años* means \"I stopped smoking two years ago\". With *no*, *no dejar de* + infinitive means to keep on doing it: *No dejo de cocinar en casa* means \"I keep cooking at home\"."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Dejar de + infinitive (indefinido)",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "yo dejé de fumar",
+          "I stopped smoking"
+        ],
+        [
+          "tú dejaste de fumar",
+          "you stopped smoking"
+        ],
+        [
+          "él, ella, usted dejó de fumar",
+          "he, she stopped / you (formal) stopped smoking"
+        ],
+        [
+          "nosotros dejamos de fumar",
+          "we stopped smoking"
+        ],
+        [
+          "vosotros dejasteis de fumar",
+          "you all stopped smoking (Spain)"
+        ],
+        [
+          "ellos, ellas, ustedes dejaron de fumar",
+          "they, you all stopped smoking"
+        ]
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "Dejé de fumar hace dos años.",
+          "english": "I stopped smoking two years ago."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Mi madre dejó de comprar productos preparados.",
+          "english": "My mother stopped buying prepared products."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Mi hermano dejó de beber refrescos y ahora bebe agua.",
+          "english": "My brother stopped drinking soft drinks and now drinks water."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Aunque es difícil, no dejo de cocinar en casa.",
+          "english": "Although it is difficult, I keep on cooking at home."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
       "content": "Use connectors to show stages, causes and contrasts so that an account of changing habits becomes a connected narrative."
diff --git a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-22-04-hypothetical-structures-gr.json b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-22-04-hypothetical-structures-gr.json
index 45f98486..5a2cb06f 100644
--- a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-22-04-hypothetical-structures-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-22-04-hypothetical-structures-gr.json
@@ -27,6 +27,61 @@
         }
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "content": "You know the simple future from A2 Unit 19 and the past participle from A2 Unit 1. The *futuro perfecto* combines them: *haber* in the future (*habré, habrás, habrá, habremos, habréis, habrán*) + the participle, which does not change. It says that something will already be finished by a point in the future: *En 2030 habremos terminado la casa.* Spanish also uses it to guess about the past: *Habrá salido ya* means \"he has probably left already\". You will meet that use later; here you practise the first."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Futuro perfecto: haber + participle",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "yo habré terminado",
+          "I will have finished"
+        ],
+        [
+          "tú habrás terminado",
+          "you will have finished"
+        ],
+        [
+          "él, ella, usted habrá terminado",
+          "he, she, you (formal) will have finished"
+        ],
+        [
+          "nosotros habremos terminado",
+          "we will have finished"
+        ],
+        [
+          "vosotros habréis terminado",
+          "you all will have finished (Spain)"
+        ],
+        [
+          "ellos, ellas, ustedes habrán terminado",
+          "they, you all will have finished"
+        ]
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "En 2030 habremos terminado la casa.",
+          "english": "By 2030 we will have finished the house."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Para el viernes habré terminado el trabajo.",
+          "english": "By Friday I will have finished the work."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Dentro de diez años, la situación habrá cambiado mucho.",
+          "english": "In ten years, the situation will have changed a lot."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "No contesta el teléfono; habrá salido ya.",
+          "english": "He is not answering the phone; he has probably left already."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
       "content": "A hypothetical answer can contain both a condition and a probability marker to show degrees of uncertainty."
diff --git a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-40-02-gr.json b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-40-02-gr.json
index 2a8689af..0c8d2294 100644
--- a/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-40-02-gr.json
+++ b/content/es-es/grammar/b1/b1-40-02-gr.json
@@ -45,6 +45,49 @@
         }
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "content": "You know *sino* and *sino que* from the table above. *No solo … sino (también)* is a pair that adds a second item to the first and gives both weight: \"not only … but also\". Before a noun or an adjective use *sino (también)*; before a new conjugated verb use *sino que (también)*. *Tanto … como* (\"both … and\") is the plain version and takes a plural verb: *Tanto mi hermano como yo vivimos aquí.*"
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "No solo … sino (también) and tanto … como",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "no solo + noun / adjective, sino (también) + noun / adjective",
+          "No solo es caro, sino también incómodo."
+        ],
+        [
+          "no solo + verb, sino que (también) + verb",
+          "No solo canta, sino que también baila."
+        ],
+        [
+          "tanto A como B (both A and B)",
+          "Tanto mi hermano como yo vivimos aquí."
+        ]
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "El examen no solo era difícil, sino también muy largo.",
+          "english": "The exam was not only difficult but also very long."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "No solo perdí el documento, sino que también olvidé el teléfono.",
+          "english": "I not only lost the document but also forgot the phone."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "No solo trabaja los sábados, sino que también estudia por la noche.",
+          "english": "He not only works on Saturdays but also studies at night."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Tanto la casa como el barrio son muy tranquilos.",
+          "english": "Both the house and the neighbourhood are very quiet."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
       "content": "Formula: Negative clause + noun/adj = **sino**. Negative clause + conjugated verb = **sino que**."
